【71†Audio】 【72†Blogs】 【73†Facts for Features】 【74†Fact Sheets】 【51†Infographics and Visualizations】 【75†Publications】 【76†Spotlights】 【77†Stats for Stories】 【39†Training (Census Academy)】 【78†Videos】 【79†Working Papers】\nL126: # Computing Apportionment\nL127: \nL128: 【0†Skip Navigation】\nL129: \nL130: 【80†Within About This Topic】\nL131: \nL132:   * 【81†Computing Apportionment】\nL133:   * 【82†Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)】\nL134:   * 【83†Historical Perspective】\nL135: \nL136: Within About This Topic\nL137: \nL138: 【80†Within About This Topic】\nL139: \nL140:   * 【81†Computing Apportionment】\nL141:   * 【82†Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)】\nL142:   * 【83†Historical Perspective】\nL143: # Computing Apportionment\nL144: \nL145: Article 1, Section 2, of the United States Constitution states:\nL146: \nL147: \"Representatives and direct Taxes shall be apportioned among the several States which may be included within this Union, according to their respective Numbers...The actual Enumeration shall be made within three Years after the first Meeting of the Congress of the United States, and within every subsequent Term of ten Years, in such Manner as they shall by Law direct.\"\nL148: Therein lies the primary mandate of the U.S. census, apportionment of the House of Representatives. Since that first census in 1790, five methods of apportionment have been used. The current method used, the Method of Equal Proportions, was adopted by congress in 1941 following the census of 1940. This method assigns seats in the House of Representatives according to a \"priority\" value. The priority value is determined by multiplying the population of a state by a \"multiplier.\"\nL149: Each of the 50 states is given one seat out of the current total of 435. The next, or 51st seat, goes to the state with the highest priority value and becomes that state's second seat. This continues until all 435 seats have been assigned to a state. This is how it is done.\nL150: ### Equal Proportions Method\nL151: \nL152: P - represents a state's total population\nL153: \nL154: n - represents the number of seats a state would have if it gained a seat (because all states automatically received one seat the next seat gained is \"seat two,\" and the next \"seat three,\" and the next \"seat four,\" and so on.)\nL155: The multiplier equals:\nL156: 【84†Image: 1 divided by the square root of n(n-1)】\nL157: [which is called the reciprocal of the geometric mean]. Computing these values is quite easy using a computer with spreadsheet software (such as Excel).\nL158: \nL159: Thus the formula for calculating the multiplier for the second seat is:\nL160: 【85†Image: 1 divided by the square root of 2(2-1)】\nL161: or 1/1.414213562 or 0.70710678\nL162: \nL163: the multiplier for the third seat is:\nL164: 【86†Image: 1 divided by the square root of 3(3-1)】\nL165: 1/2.449489743 or 0.40824829\nL166: the multiplier for the fourth seat is:\nL167: 【87†Image: 1 divided by the square root of 4(4-1)】\nL168: 1/3.464101615 or 0.288675134\nL169: \nL170: Continue until an appropriate number of multipliers have been calculated.\nL171: Once the \"multipliers\" have been calculated, the next step is to multiply this figure by the population total for each of the 50 states (the District of Columbia is not included in these calculations). The resulting numbers are the priority values. Make sure you compute enough multipliers and priority values to cover the largest number of seats in the U.S. House of Representatives that any one state stands to gain.\nL172: For example, if the largest number of seats currently assigned to a state is 60, multipliers and priority values must be calculated for at least the 60th seat. If you are using a computer, you should compute multipliers for seats 2 through 70. This will assure you have enough multipliers and priority values for apportionment.\nL173: Once you've calculated priority values for the total number of potential seats for each state, the next step is to rank and assign seat numbers to the resulting priority values starting with seat 51, until all 435 seats have been assigned (remember, each state automatically received one seat). Next, tally the number of seats for each state to arrive at the total number of seats in the House of Representatives apportioned to each state.\nL174: \nL175: Page Last Revised - November 22, 2021\nL176: \nL177: ✕\nL178: Is this page helpful?\nL179: 【88†Image: Thumbs Up Image†census.gov】 Yes 【89†Image: Thumbs Down Image†census.gov】 No\nL180: \nL181: ✕\nL182: \nL183: [Input]\nL184: \nL185: NO THANKS\nL186: \nL187: 255 characters maximum 【90†Image†census.gov】255 characters maximum reached\nL188: \nL189: ✕\nL190: \nL191: Thank you for your feedback.\nL192: Comments or suggestions? 【91†Image†census.gov】【92†Image†census.gov】\nL193: \nL194: Top\nL195: \nL196: 【93†Back to Header】\nL197: \nL198: Receive Updates\nL199: \nL200: To sign up for updates please enter your email address.\nL201: \nL202: * * *\nL203: \nL204: Stay Current\nL205: 【70†America Counts】 【94†Director’s Blog】 【95†Deputy Director’s Blog】 【96†Random Samplings】 【97†Research Matters】\nL206: \nL207: Stay Connected\nL208: \nL209: 【9†Contact】 【10†Support】 【7†Jobs】\nL210: \nL211: Follow\nL212: \nL213: 【98†Information Quality】 | 【99†Data Linkage Infrastructure】 | 【100†Data Protection and Privacy Policy】 | 【101†Accessibility】 | 【102†FOIA】 | 【103†Inspector General†www.oig.doc.gov】 | 【104†No FEAR Act†www.commerce.gov】 | 【105†U.S. Department of Commerce†www.commerce.gov】 | 【106†USA.gov†www.usa.gov】\nL214: \nL215: * * *\nL216: Measuring America's People and Economy\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2023 Review | Boundary Commission for England (https://boundarycommissionforengland.independent.gov.uk/2023-review/)\nciteturn26view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://boundarycommissionforengland.independent.gov.uk/2023-review/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 128\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Boundary Commission for England】\nL3: \nL4: Search this site [Input: Search...] Search\nL5: \nL6:   * 【1†Home】\nL7:   * 【2†2023 Review】\nL8:   * 【3†News】\nL9:   * 【4†Blogs】\nL10:   * 【5†Data and Resources】\nL11:   * 【6†About us】\nL12: \nL13: # 2023 Review\nL14: ## Final report and recommendations\nL15: We have now concluded the 2023 Review of Parliamentary constituencies in England, and submitted our final report and recommendations. You can access via the links below the content of that report and recommendations, together with various data files. You can also view an interactive map of the recommendations at 【7†bcereviews.org.uk†bcereviews.org.uk】.\nL16: The Government will draft an Order containing the recommendations of all four Parliamentary Boundary Commissions: once that draft Order is approved by the Privy Council, the new constituencies will be used for the next General election following that date (for any by-election that may take place beforehand, existing constituencies are used).\nL17:   * 【8†Volume one: Report】\nL18:   * 【9†Volume two: Constituency names, designations and composition】\nL19:   * 【10†Volume three: Maps】\nL20:   * 【11†2023 Review final recommendations map using Google Maps】\nL21:   * 【7†Interactive map at bcereviews.org.uk†bcereviews.org.uk】\nL22:   * 【12†Final recommendations key facts document in pdf】; 【13†or in Excel】\nL23:   * 【14†Final recommendations local authority ward data】\nL24:   * 【15†Representations received during the review】\nL25: \nL26: Geospatial data files:\nL27:   * All England – 【16†SHP】; 【17†GPKG】\nL28:   * East Midlands – 【18†SHP】; 【19†GPKG】; 【20†KML】\nL29:   * Eastern – 【21†SHP】; 【22†GPKG】; 【23†KML】\nL30:   * London – 【24†SHP】; 【25†GPKG】; 【26†KML】\nL31:   * North East – 【27†SHP】; 【28†GPKG】; 【29†KML】\nL32:   * North West – 【30†SHP】; 【31†GPKG】; 【32†KML】\nL33:   * South East – 【33†SHP】; 【34†GPKG】; 【35†KML】\nL34:   * South West – 【36†SHP】; 【37†GPKG】; 【38†KML】\nL35:   * West Midlands – 【39†SHP】; 【40†GPKG】; 【41†KML】\nL36:   * Yorkshire and the Humber – 【42†SHP】; 【43†GPKG】; 【44†KML】\nL37: The new constituency boundaries shown here are indicative and are subject to confirmation by Ordnance Survey. The final boundaries may have small differences.\nL38: \nL39: Please note that the Keyhole Markup Language (KML) files have been simplified to a 10 metre accuracy in order to keep the file sizes within Google Map limits.\nL40: If you wish to view historic consultation documentation from earlier in the review, please click on the link for the English region you are interested in: 【45†East Midlands】; 【46†Eastern】; 【47†London】; 【48†North East】; 【49†North West】; 【50†South East】; 【51†South West】; 【52†West Midlands】; 【53†Yorkshire and the Humber】. All-England files are accessible from the relevant sections below.\nL41: ## [Button: England-wide data from Revised proposals]\nL42: \nL43:   * 【54†Electorate breakdown for revised proposed constituencies】;\nL44:   * 【55†Each revised proposed constituency with its region and sub-region】;\nL45:   * 【56†SHP geospatial data】;\nL46:   * 【57†GPKG geospatial data】.\nL47: ## [Button: England-wide data from Initial proposals]\nL48: \nL49:   * 【58†Electorate breakdown for initial proposed constituencies】;\nL50:   * 【59†Each initial proposed constituency with its region and sub-region】;\nL51:   * 【60†SHP geospatial data】;\nL52:   * 【61†GPKG geospatial data】.\nL53: ## [Button: General information about the 2023 Review]\nL54: \nL55: Following the passing of the 【62†Parliamentary Constituencies Act 2020†www.legislation.gov.uk】 in December 2020, and the publication of the relevant 【63†Parliamentary electorate data†www.ons.gov.uk】 in January 2021, we began a new review of all Parliamentary constituencies in England. We refer to this as the ‘2023 Review’, as we were required to report with our final recommendations by 1 July 2023.\nL56: Applying the statutory formula to the electorate figures meant the total 650 constituencies were to be distributed during the review to the four parts of the UK as follows:\nL57: \nL58:   * England = 543 (includes two ‘protected’ constituencies on the Isle of Wight);\nL59:   * Scotland = 57 (includes two ‘protected’ constituencies for specified Scottish islands);\nL60:   * Wales = 32 (includes one ‘protected’ constituency on the Isle of Anglesey); and\nL61:   * Northern Ireland = 18\nL62: This Commission applied the same distribution formula to the English allocation, which resulted in the following redistribution of constituencies among the nine English regions for the 2023 Review:\nL63:   * East Midlands = 47 (increase of one)\nL64:   * Eastern = 61 (increase of three)\nL65:   * London = 75 (increase of two)\nL66:   * North East = 27 (decrease of two)\nL67:   * North West = 73 (decrease of two)\nL68:   * South East = 91 (increase of seven)\nL69:   * South West = 58 (increase of three)\nL70:   * West Midlands = 57 (decrease of two)\nL71:   * Yorkshire and the Humber = 54 (no change)\nL72: Application of further statutory rules to the published electorate also meant that all recommended constituencies had to have no less than 69,724 Parliamentary electors and no more than 77,062 (except those ‘protected’ constituencies mentioned above). By law, these electorate figures had to be the electorates as they were on 2 March 2020.\nL73: ### What happens next?\nL74: Submission of our final report and recommendations to the Speaker of the House of Commons concludes our involvement in the 2023 Boundary Review. Once the reports of all four Parliamentary Boundary Commissons have been laid before Parliament fby the Speaker, the Government must prepare a draft Order to implement the new constituencies for the whole UK.\nL75: This should be provided to the Privy Council for approval within four months of the last report of the four Parliamentary Boundary Commissions for the UK being laid in Parliament. Once the Privy Council approves the Order, the new constituencies will be used at the next General election following that date (any by-election in the meantime will continue to use the existing constituency).\nL76: ### What ward-level electorate data did you use?\nL77: Mostly the data published by the Office for National Statistics in January 2021. That ONS publication reflected wards as they were at the time of the data collection, but December 2020 legislation introduced the concept of our work taking account of ‘prospective’ local government boundaries, i.e. wards that had – at the operative date of 1 December 2020 – been ‘made’ by legal instrument, but not yet implemented at a subsequent local election.\nL78: Nearly 10% of local authorities in England (mostly in London) had such ‘prospective’ ward boundaries, specifically:  1) Barnet; 2) Basingstoke and Deane; 3) Brent; 4) Buckinghamshire; 5) Cambridge; 6) Camden; 7) Chorley; 8) Cornwall; 9) Ealing; 10) Enfield; 11) Halton; 12) Hammersmith and Fulham; 13) Haringey; 14) Harrow; 15) Hartlepool; 16) Hillingdon; 17) Hounslow; 18) Isle of Wight; 19) Islington; 20) Lewisham; 21) Merton; 22) North Northamptonshire; 23) Oxford; 24) Pendle; 25) Richmond upon Thames; 26) Rotherham; 27) Salford; 28) Sutton; 29) West Northamptonshire; 30) Westminster; 31) Wiltshire.\nL79: We therefore worked with the Electoral Registration Officers for all these local authorities, to recast the ONS data according to the prospective wards: you can download the complete ward-level dataset used for the review from the 2023 Review section of our Data and Resources page.\nL80: ### What were your process and policies for the 2023 Review?\nL81: \nL82: The review progressed as follows:\nL83:   * 5 Jan 2021: Publication of headline electorate figures by ONS, BCE begin development of initial proposals;\nL84:   * 24 March 2021: Publish complete ward-level electorate figures (i.e. including ‘prospective’ wards);\nL85:   * 10 May 2021: Publish ‘Guide to the 2023 Review’;\nL86:   * 8 June 2021: Publish initial proposals and conduct eight-week written consultation;\nL87:   * 22 February 2022: Publish responses to initial proposals and conduct six-week ‘secondary consultation’, including between two and five public hearings in each region;\nL88:   * 8 November 2022: Publish revised proposals and conduct four-week written consultation;\nL89:   * 27 June 2023: Submit and publish final report and recommendations.\nL90: You can read more about the process, law and policies in our 【64†Guide to the 2023 Review】.\nL91: \nL92: To assist with promoting awareness and engagement with the review, we created and supplied a partner pack, which included our press release, blog, copy and graphics that could be used on websites or social media channels, an FAQ and more. 【65†Download our partner pack here】.\nL93: All information about the review was published via this section of our website, with key updates and notifications also issued via our Twitter account 【66†@BCEReviews†twitter.com】.\nL94: \nL95: This page reformats automatically when printed. 【67†Print this section】\nL96: ## News\nL97: \nL98: #### 【68†Annual electorate publication】\nL99: \nL100: 31 July, 2024\nL101: \nL102: #### 【69†The Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023】\nL103: \nL104: 20 November, 2023\nL105: \nL106: #### 【70†Boundary Commission for England publishes final recommendations for new Parliamentary constituencies】\nL107: \nL108: 28 June, 2023\nL109: \nL110: #### 【71†Boundary Commission submits final recommendations for new Parliamentary constituencies to the Speaker】\nL111: \nL112: 27 June, 2023\nL113: #### 【72†On track to deliver the final recommendations】\nL114: \nL115: 31 May, 2023\nL116: \nL117:   * 【73†Accessibility】\nL118:   * 【74†Data Protection and Privacy】\nL119:   * 【75†Cookies】\nL120:   * 【76†Freedom of Information】\nL121:   * 【77†Contact us】\nL122: \nL123: 【78†Crown Copyright 2023†www.nationalarchives.gov.uk】\nL124: \nL125: ## Follow us\nL126: \nL127:   *   *   *\n"
